ed Multisystem Disorder)", "posterior": 0.235, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "The lack of classical cystic fibrosis features weakens the specific diagnosis of CFTR-related disease (B1.1) but preser…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B1.2", "B2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=40%, source=primekg]\n\nCystic Fibrosis (CFTR-Related Multisystem Disorder) vs Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD) with Chronic Airway Disease:\n  Favours first:  biliary cirrhosis, childhood onset, cirrhosis, clubbing of fingers, cor pulmonale\n  Favours second: abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n\nNOT typically seen in Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD) with Chronic Airway Disease: abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal sweat electrolytes, decreased fertility, heterotaxy\n\nRelated to Cystic Fibrosis (CFTR-Related Multisystem Disorder): autosomal recessive disease, respiratory system disease\n\nRelated to Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD) with Chronic Airway Disease: ciliary discoordination due to random ciliary orientation, ciliary dyskinesia with defective radial spokes, ciliary dyskinesia with excessively long cilia\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 10-year-old girl is admitted to the medical floor for a respiratory infection → recurrent bronchopulmonary infections → Cystic Fibrosis (CFTR-Related Multisystem Disorder)\n  A 10-year-old girl is admitted to the medical floor for a respiratory infection → recurrent respiratory infections → Cystic Fibro